## CRITICAL LIMITATION: COGNITIVE TRAINING TRANSFER EFFECTS

**The Transfer Problem** (2024-2025 Meta-Analyses):
- Working memory training improves trained task performance in healthy adults with small effect size; utility of this type of training in real-life improvements in cognition may be minimal
- 12-session process-based working memory training showed training effect (improved trained task) only in young adults; transfer of WM training effects was not revealed
- 8-week adaptive n-back training in healthy middle-aged adults (n=55): strong and consistent evidence for absence of near and far transfer effects in untrained cognitive tasks despite neuroimaging showing changes

**Exception (MCI Population)**:
- Cognitive training in MCI older adults showed significant effects in both trained (working memory) and untrained domains (executive function, ability of daily living); effects on overall cognition, working memory, and daily life ability were maintained for at least 3 months post-training

**Conclusion**: Cognitive training benefits those with cognitive decline (MCI, post-stroke) but shows limited real-world transfer in healthy adults. Prioritize multimodal interventions over isolated cognitive training.

