### 6.1 Novel 2026 Findings Integration

**Glymphatic System Optimization** (February 2026):
The discovery that physical exercise enhances CSF-driven waste clearance through aquaporin-4 polarization and vascular pulsatility improvements adds mechanistic proof to why multimodal interventions (especially aerobic exercise) matter. Glymphatic dysfunction is now recognized as contributor to cognitive decline and neurodegeneration.

**Head & Neck Massage for Amyloid Reduction** (January 2026):
Preliminary mouse data (University of Arizona, SfN 2025) suggests twice-daily head/neck massage improved cognitive function, reduced amyloid, and lowered neurodegeneration biomarkers in Alzheimer's model mice. Human trial planned. Mechanism: Enhanced lymphatic drainage via mechanical stimulation.

**CO₂ Breathing for Cerebral Vasomotion** (January 2026):
Neuropsychologist Sephira Ryman (University of New Mexico) demonstrated older adults and Parkinson's disease patients increased brain vasomotion and cerebrospinal fluid flow by breathing dilute carbon dioxide (dilates blood vessels) for short periods. Implication: Non-pharmacological vascular enhancement possible.

**Dietary Patterns & Long-Term Cognition** (March 2026):
JAMA Neurology study (March 2026) published findings underscoring potential of healthy diet to maintain long-term cognitive health; highlights need for implementation research to translate insights into scalable interventions. MIND & Mediterranean diet continue supported.

