  → Extend sleep window ±30 minutes (sleep duration may be insufficient)
  → Reduce caffeine cutoff to 16 hours pre-bedtime (ultra-sensitivity)
  → Add magnesium L-threonate 144 mg 2 hours pre-sleep (consult MD)
  → Rule out sleep apnea, restless legs (ask MD for sleep study)
  → Reduce evening blue light exposure earlier (3 hours pre-bed)
  → Avoid alcohol (disrupts REM, even 1 drink impacts sleep architecture)

PROBLEM: "Exercise leaves me exhausted; I can't maintain 3 sessions/week"
SOLUTIONS:
  → Reduce duration to 15 minutes (build up gradually)
  → Reduce intensity to 55–60% max HR (overly intense Week 1 is common)
  → Check sleep sufficiency (exercise recovery depends on sleep)
  → Verify hydration (even mild dehydration impairs recovery)
  → Space sessions ≥48 hours apart (not enough recovery time)
  → Check nutrition (carbs post-exercise support recovery)

PROBLEM: "I can't achieve 11/15 MIND diet points"
SOLUTIONS:
  → Start with easiest 5 points (leafy greens + olive oil + whole grains + nuts + berries)
  → Simplify: Use frozen vegetables (same nutrient profile, less prep)
  → Use grocery delivery to reduce friction
  → Meal prep Sunday evening (batch cook vegetables, grains for week)
  → If fish unavailable: algae omega-3 supplement instead
  → Don't aim for perfection; 10/15 (67%) still provides benefit

PROBLEM: "Can't meditate for 20 minutes; mind won't settle"
SOLUTIONS:
  → Start with 5 minutes; extend by 1 minute weekly (gradual adaptation)
  → Use guided meditation apps (external structure helps focus)
  → Try body scan instead of breath focus (some respond better to somatic input)
  → Meditate after exercise (tired mind easier to focus)
  → Meditate in same location daily (environmental cue)
  → Reduce sensory distractions (quiet room, earplugs if needed)

PROBLEM: "Started intermittent fasting Week 5; feeling fatigued"
SOLUTIONS:
  → STOP immediately (fasting is optional, not essential)
  → Return to normal eating windows for 1 week
  → Reassess sleep & exercise adherence (IF demands better foundations)
  → Consider earlier eating window (e.g., 10 AM–6 PM vs. 12 PM–8 PM)
  → Try 5:2 (calorie restriction 2 days/week) instead of daily IF
  → Reintroduce IF once foundations solid AND fatigue resolved

PROBLEM: "Week 8 assessment: All metrics good, but cognitive testing flat"
SOLUTIONS:
  → Cognitive improvements plateau Week 6–8 (ceiling effect in early responders)
  → Retest Week 12 (12-week minimum for significant cognitive change)
  → Ensure practice effects controlled (different test forms each session)
  → Check sleep architecture still optimal (most common cause of plateau)
  → Add cognitive training component (if available; improves trained task)
  → Verify diet adherence (diet slippage common Week 5–8)

